{
  "name": "What's the Best Way to Fix a Large Hole in My Wall?",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Tackling a large hole in your drywall can seem daunting, but with the right steps, you can get it looking good as new. First, you'll want to inspect the area carefully to make sure there are no studs or wires directly behind where you'll be cutting. The goal is to create clean, straight edges around the damaged area, so you might need to use a utility knife and a straightedge. For larger holes, you'll definitely need a patch with backing strips to provide support for the new drywall piece. Once your new drywall is cut to fit, you'll install it and then apply joint tape or mesh over all the seams. This is a critical step for strength and to prevent future cracks. Next comes the joint compound: apply it in thin, even coats, making sure to feather the edges out smoothly. You'll need to let each coat dry fully—often around 24 hours—and sand lightly between coats to achieve a truly seamless finish. Don't rush this part; patience is your friend! Finally, prime the patched area before painting to ensure the new paint blends perfectly with the rest of your wall. If it feels like too much, or you're aiming for a perfect, invisible repair, the team at HH Remodeling LLC is here to help with professional drywall patching across the Denver area.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
